  <h2 id="edge">Where the house edge comes from</h2>
  <p>Blackjack looks fair — you and the dealer draw from the same cards. The catch is the order. <strong>You act first</strong>, and if you bust you lose even if the dealer busts later. That rule alone is worth about 8% to the house.</p>
  <p>You win most of it back through things the dealer can’t do: get paid 3 to 2 on blackjack, double, split, and stand when it suits you. Do all of that correctly and the house edge shrinks to <strong>0.3–0.7%</strong> depending on the rules. Mimic the dealer (hit to 17) and it’s around 5.5%.</p>

  <h2 id="works">What works: 5 things that cut your losses</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Learn basic strategy.</strong> By far the biggest factor. Use the <a href={routes.strategy.en}>interactive chart</a> for your table’s rules and practice until you don’t hesitate.</li>
    <li><strong>Play only 3:2 tables.</strong> A 6:5 payout costs about 1.4 percentage points — more than all your mistakes combined.</li>
    <li><strong>Skip insurance.</strong> It’s a side bet with roughly a 7% house edge.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the rules.</strong> Dealer stands on soft 17, double after split and surrender all help you. Fewer decks beat more.</li>
    <li><strong>Set a limit before you sit down</strong> and keep it. Blackjack swings are big: you’ll have losing streaks even playing perfectly.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="doesnt">What doesn’t work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Betting systems</strong> (Martingale, Paroli, 1-3-2-6…): they change bet sizes, not the odds of any hand. The house edge applies to every dollar you wager.</li>
    <li><strong>Hot and cold tables:</strong> the cards don’t remember whether you won the last hand.</li>
    <li><strong>“Never bust” (stand on 12+):</strong> sounds safe, costs money against strong dealer cards.</li>
    <li><strong>Playing like the dealer:</strong> one of the worst strategies there is.</li>
    <li><strong>“Winning systems” sold online:</strong> if they worked, nobody would sell them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="counting">What about counting cards?</h2>
  <p>It’s the only legal technique that can give the player an edge, and it’s real: dealt cards don’t go back into the shoe. But the edge is small (about 0.5–1%), it requires big bet swings at the right moments, iron discipline and a large bankroll — and casinos shuffle early or show you the door once they notice. It doesn’t work online. The full story is in <a href={routes.counting.en}>how to count cards</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="mindset">A healthy way to look at it</h2>
  <p>Treat blackjack like any paid entertainment: a concert ticket costs money and you don’t expect it back. The difference is that in blackjack you choose how much it costs — and playing perfectly keeps the price minimal.</p>
  <p>Learning to play well is also a genuinely satisfying mental challenge: knowing exactly why you hit 12 against a 2 or split eights against a 10. That’s what Veintiuno teaches, with no real money and no betting.</p>
